      "desc": "Facilities, general.",
      "narrative": "On May 28, 2026, a male Speke\u2019s gazelle sustained severe injuries after entering an adjacent off exhibit enclosure\ncontaining two zebras through a broken fence. The gazelle had been placed in an off exhibit enclosure adjacent to the\nzebras due to sparring behavior observed that morning. Two fence slats between the enclosures had been broken in\nSeptember 2025, removed, and never repaired. Although hay bales had been placed over the opening, the gazelle was\nstill able to access the zebra enclosure due to the missing fence slats. The area supervisor did not submit or follow up on\na work order to correct the broken enclosure fence. The gazelle received medical treatment but continued to decline and\nwas humanely euthanized. Failure to maintain housing facilities in good repair can lead to escape and injury of the\nanimals housed within. The facility must be constructed of such material and of such strength as appropriate for the\nanimals involved. The indoor and outdoor housing facilities shall be maintained in good repair to protect the animals from\ninjury and to contain the animals. The removed fence slats were fixed by the facility the morning of the incident prior to\nthis inspection.\nThis inspection and exit interview were conducted with the facility representative and attending veterinarian.\nn"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": "3.125(a)  Critical\n   \nFacilities, general. \nOn May 28, 2026, a male Speke\u2019s gazelle sustained severe injuries after entering an adjacent off exhibit enclosure \ncontaining two zebras through a broken fence. The gazelle had been placed in an off exhibit enclosure adjacent to the \nzebras due to sparring behavior observed that morning. Two fence slats between the enclosures had been broken in \nSeptember 2025, removed, and never repaired. Although hay bales had been placed over the opening, the gazelle was \nstill able to access the zebra enclosure due to the missing fence slats. The area supervisor did not submit or follow up on \na work order to correct the broken enclosure fence. The gazelle received medical treatment but continued to decline and \nwas humanely euthanized. Failure to maintain housing facilities in good repair can lead to escape and injury of the \nanimals housed within. The facility must be constructed of such material and of such strength as appropriate for the \nanimals involved. The indoor and outdoor housing facilities shall be maintained in good repair to protect the animals from \ninjury and to contain the animals. The removed fence slats were fixed by the facility the morning of the incident prior to \nthis inspection. \n \nThis inspection and exit interview were conducted with the facility representative and attending veterinarian. \n \n n",
